00:17:50.297 --> 00:17:51.258
<v Maya>So you learn even less.</v>

158
00:17:51.618 --> 00:17:57.823
<v Daniel>The capability gap between what you can do with the tool versus what you can do without it would turn into an absolute chasm.</v>

159
00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:09.115
<v Maya>Which brings us to the real world implications of all this. Let's start with labor market policy. You hear politicians and executives constantly talking about upskilling workers whose jobs are threatened by automation.</v>

160
00:18:09.495 --> 00:18:10.255
<v Daniel>You hear it all the time.</v>

161
00:18:10.495 --> 00:18:17.118
<v Maya>But based on this paper, is handing a worker an AI tool a true long term upskilling policy or is it just a temporary fix?</v>

162
00:18:17.587 --> 00:18:29.474
<v Daniel>It looks much more like a temporary fix, a capability rental. If a government or a corporation wants true upskilling, they can't just buy everyone a chat GPT enterprise license and consider the job done.</v>

163
00:18:29.514 --> 00:18:30.814
<v Maya>Check the box. We're upskilled.</v>

164
00:18:31.014 --> 00:18:45.981
<v Daniel>Right. That doesn't work. They have to invest in foundational knowledge building. Because otherwise, the moment that subscription ends or the Internet goes down or more realistically, the company needs an unassisted human to make a critical, nuanced judgment call</v>

165
00:18:46.381 --> 00:18:49.962
<v Daniel>that the AI is failing at. That worker is right back to square one.</v>

166
00:18:50.383 --> 00:19:01.687
<v Maya>OK, what about team design? If I am a manager listening to this right now, how do I actually structure a team knowing that my juniors have raw capability but zero foundational knowledge?</v>

167
00:19:02.492 --> 00:19:12.077
<v Daniel>The authors suggest a fascinating shift in how we hire and build teams. Historically, you might hire a pyramid of mid-level coders supervised by a senior developer.</v>

168
00:19:12.097 --> 00:19:13.198
<v Maya>Right, the classic structure.</v>

169
00:19:13.718 --> 00:19:28.666
<v Daniel>But now you might pair several AI-enabled generalists, people like the consultants in this study, who can punch way above their weight class using the tool, to generate the bulk of the raw code. And you supervise them with one deep foundational expert.</v>

170
00:19:29.498 --> 00:19:36.122
<v Maya>OK, let's break down how that actually functions day to day. What is the expert doing in that scenario?</v>

171
00:19:36.522 --> 00:19:50.270
<v Daniel>The expert isn't writing the code anymore. Their job is entirely supervisory and critical. They are reading the AI generated code to look for landmines. They spot the subtle hallucinations and they provide the calibration that the generalists lack.</v>

172
00:19:51.047 --> 00:19:56.389
<v Maya>It's like having a construction crew where everyone is wearing an exoskeleton, wildly throwing steel beams around.</v>

173
00:19:56.409 --> 00:19:57.089
