174
00:19:57.269 --> 00:20:04.971
<v Maya>You absolutely need one master engineer on site without a suit, just watching to make sure they don't accidentally pull the entire building down.</v>

175
00:20:05.172 --> 00:20:17.916
<v Daniel>That is the exact dynamic. And finally, think about the implications for education, for universities teaching technical skills right now. Is mere capability with a tool a valid skill to teach in grade?</v>

176
00:20:18.356 --> 00:20:19.076
<v Maya>That's a tough one.</v>

177
00:20:19.752 --> 00:20:31.878
<v Daniel>If a student can orchestrate an AI to solve a complex predictive model, do they get an A? Or is that just a rental? And the university's true job is to force them to take the exoskeleton off and do the actual math.</v>

178
00:20:32.458 --> 00:20:40.085
<v Maya>It forces a massive philosophical shift in what a degree even means. Okay, let's bring this all together. We went deep today.</v>

179
00:20:40.286 --> 00:20:40.806
<v Daniel>We really did.</v>

180
00:20:41.266 --> 00:20:55.954
<v Maya>We started with Emma Wiles' brand new paper showing that management consultants could perform like junior data scientists when given generative AI, closing a massive 63-point skill gap. But the moment the AI was removed, their knowledge simply vanished.</v>

181
00:20:56.715 --> 00:21:11.656
<v Daniel>And we saw how this perfectly aligns with the Otter Patent Lawyer Study, confirming that true expertise takes time and foundations to build. We explored how operating entirely outside your skill set causes a dangerous overconfidence, flipping the script on the</v>

182
00:21:11.756 --> 00:21:15.202
<v Daniel>expert skepticism we saw in Agarwal's radiology study.</v>

183
00:21:15.939 --> 00:21:30.845
<v Maya>And we broke down the learning mechanism with the Bastani high school math study. We realized that to actually learn from AI, it needs to act as a personal trainer that introduces cognitive friction, not a crutch that just carries you across the finish line. Generative</v>

184
00:21:30.965 --> 00:21:44.390
<v Maya>AI is an incredible tool that allows non-experts to punch vastly above their weight class. But we cannot confuse enhanced output with genuine human learning. It is an exoskeleton, not a muscle builder.</v>

185
00:21:44.984 --> 00:21:51.483
<v Daniel>Understanding the difference between renting a capability and owning a skill will really define the successful knowledge worker of the next decade.</v>

186
00:21:52.187 --> 00:22:06.976
<v Maya>Which leaves you, our listener, with a final thought to mull over. If we are entering an era where hard technical skills like Python coding or statistical modeling can be successfully and cheaply rented just by putting on an AI exoskeleton,</v>

187
00:22:07.657 --> 00:22:22.410
<v Maya>perhaps the most valuable human skill of the future isn't technical proficiency at all. Perhaps the ultimate skill is simply calibration, the wisdom to know exactly what you don't know so you never blindly trust the machine. So the next time you put on the</v>

188
00:22:22.451 --> 00:22:29.301
<v Maya>digital costume, just make sure you know who is actually doing the lifting. Thanks for joining us on the deep dive and keep learning.</v>
